In this appendix we analyze the mathematical properties of the canonical 
rendering function


\[
R(\psi) = \big( H(\psi),\, S(\psi),\, V(\psi) \big),
\]


where


\[
H(\psi) = \frac{\arg(\psi)}{2\pi}, \qquad
S(\psi) = \frac{|\psi|}{\max |\psi|}, \qquad
V(\psi) = |\psi|^\gamma.
\]



\subsection{Domain and Codomain}

The rendering map acts on a complex-valued projected field


\[
\psi : \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{C},
\]


and outputs a point in HSV color space:


\[
R : \mathbb{C} \to [0,1]^3.
\]



\subsection{Phase Invariance}

Let $\psi$ undergo a global phase shift:


\[
\psi \mapsto e^{i\theta} \psi.
\]



Then


\[
H(e^{i\theta}\psi)
=
H(\psi) + \frac{\theta}{2\pi} \mod 1,
\]


while


\[
S(e^{i\theta}\psi) = S(\psi),
\qquad
V(e^{i\theta}\psi) = V(\psi).
\]



Thus the rendering preserves:

\begin{itemize}
    \item amplitude structure,
    \item contrast,
    \item saturation,
\end{itemize}

and encodes phase as hue.

\subsection{Amplitude Monotonicity}

The saturation and value channels satisfy


\[
\frac{\partial S}{\partial |\psi|} > 0,
\qquad
\frac{\partial V}{\partial |\psi|} > 0.
\]



Thus brighter and more saturated regions correspond to larger field 
magnitudes.

\subsection{Contrast Control}

The exponent $\gamma$ controls contrast:


\[
V(\psi) = |\psi|^\gamma.
\]



\begin{itemize}
    \item $\gamma < 1$: high contrast, highlights low-amplitude structure.
    \item $\gamma = 1$: linear brightness.
    \item $\gamma > 1$: low contrast, emphasizes high-amplitude regions.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Smoothness}

The rendering map is smooth on $\mathbb{C} \setminus \{0\}$:


\[
R \in C^\infty(\mathbb{C} \setminus \{0\}).
\]



At $\psi = 0$, the phase is undefined, but the value and saturation 
remain continuous:


\[
S(0) = 0,
\qquad
V(0) = 0.
\]



\subsection{Structure Preservation}

Let $\psi = f(r)\Theta(\hat{x})$ be the projection of a canonical 
universe.  
Then:

\begin{itemize}
    \item radial structure is preserved in $V(\psi)$,
    \item angular structure is preserved in $H(\psi)$,
    \item relative mode amplitudes are preserved in $S(\psi)$.
\end{itemize}

Thus the rendering map faithfully encodes the geometry of the 
canonical universe.

\subsection{Invariance Under Canonical Transformations}

The rendering function is invariant under:

\begin{itemize}
    \item global phase shifts,
    \item uniform amplitude rescaling (after normalization),
    \item $SO(4)$ rotations of the underlying universe.
\end{itemize}

Therefore, visualizations reflect intrinsic structure rather than 
coordinate artifacts.

\subsection{Conclusion}

The canonical rendering function is:

\begin{itemize}
