    \item gradient coupling: $\nabla\phi \cdot \nabla\chi$,
    \item harmonic mixing: $Y_{\ell m}^\phi Y_{\ell' m'}^\chi$.
\end{itemize}

Coupled canonical universes satisfy a joint stability condition.

\subsection{Quantization}

A quantum version of the canonical universe is obtained by promoting 
$\phi$ to an operator:


\[
\hat{\phi}(x,t),
\qquad
[\hat{\phi}(x), \hat{\pi}(y)] = i\delta(x-y).
\]



The canonical PDE becomes a Heisenberg equation:


\[
\frac{d\hat{\phi}}{dt}
=
i[\hat{H},\hat{\phi}].
\]



The attractor $\phi_\ast$ becomes the expectation value of a coherent 
state:


\[
\phi_\ast(x) = \langle \alpha | \hat{\phi}(x) | \alpha \rangle.
\]



\subsection{Generalized Recursion Symmetry}

The recursion operator may be generalized to nonlinear scalings:


\[
S_\lambda \phi(x)
=
\phi(\lambda^\beta x),
\qquad
\beta \in \mathbb{R}.
\]



Or to functional recursion:


\[
S[\phi](x)
=
\phi\!\left(
x + \epsilon \, G(\phi(x))
\right).
\]



These generate fractal-like canonical universes.

